WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:06.550
Today, we are off to Colorado,

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:08.760
where there's one of the biggest

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.143
coronavirus testing experiments there is.

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:17.280
A husband and wife team,
Lou Reese and Mei Mei Hu,

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.650
happen to be executives
at a biotech company

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:22.666
which is trying to test
every single person

00:00:22.666 --> 00:00:27.440
in San Miguel County in Colorado,
which includes Telluride.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:29.030
The company's also trying to develop

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:30.370
a vaccine for the virus,

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:34.377
so let's head out and
talk to Lou and Mei Mei.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:50.030
Your company makes vaccines
for animals and for humans.

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:51.970
You've been working on a vaccine

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:54.060
for Alzheimer's and Parkinson's.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.060
Yeah, so, if it were
any other time, Ashlee,

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.960
you and I would be talking right now

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.580
about how we're gonna move forward

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:01.770
with our registrational trials

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.480
for Alzheimer's in the
States, but instead,

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.410
today we're all hands-on deck on COVID.

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:12.990
You're in Telluride, it's
part of San Miguel County,

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:15.380
and you have a test, a blood test,

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:18.450
and you're looking to
test all 8,000 people

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.310
in San Miguel County, is that the basics?

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:23.453
Yeah, testing is such a big issue.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:26.880
If you look at the countries

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.020
that have kind of dealt with this the best

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.000
is testing is one of the core principles.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.370
And what it does is it brings knowledge.

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.050
A lot of the tests that we read about

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:36.883
in the news right now,

00:01:36.883 --> 00:01:40.870
it's like the nasal swab
and it's mostly looking at

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:44.420
do you have COVID-19
right now, in this moment.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:47.160
But you have a blood test which shows

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:48.460
not only if you have it now,

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:50.470
but also if you've already had it

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:53.030
and developed antibodies against it.

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:56.080
So, antibody tests are what
we use to screen blood banks,

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.303
for instance, it's the
same type of technology.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:02.620
So, people come in, they
get their blood drawn.

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:03.590
Those all get collected,

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:05.450
and then those samples, those specimens,

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:08.543
get flown to labs to then process.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.650
And then, the lab reports the
results back to the county.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.060
The idea is how do you screen

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:23.210
large swaths of populations quickly,

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:24.900
find and trace down the ones

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.990
that are potentially
infected, to quarantine them,

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:29.380
and then also find the ones
that have developed antibodies,

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:32.190
some level of immunity,
and get them back out.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:33.620
That's the trick.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:36.670
You're in this part of Colorado
that's super spread out.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:37.820
I think there's like,

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:41.038
two and a half public health officials

00:02:41.038 --> 00:02:43.540
for these 8,000 people.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:44.720
So, it's tricky, right?

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:46.570
'Cause I know they were
looking for volunteers.

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:49.060
I mean, you have to teach
people how to do a blood draw.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:52.660
And so it played out pretty smoothly?

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:55.900
Has it been even harder
than you thought, or?

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:57.450
One of the reasons why we did it here,

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.610
in San Miguel County was because

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.060
there's not even a hospital
in San Miguel County.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:06.410
But, the medical community
here, and the EMTs,

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:08.860
knew what they were doing with
the blood drawing component.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:12.750
Also, we did it here because
it's a visionary community,

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:15.700
and I think that the community
saw what the opportunity was

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:17.530
to get the data, and it was hilarious

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.030
how awesome and supportive they've been.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:21.510
You guys are doing this for free,

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:23.610
and paying for all these tests.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.470
And then, I should add, it's voluntary.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:26.840
It's not like people have to do this.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:28.370
But do we know when, like,

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:29.930
what's your goal for
how long this will take,

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:31.660
and how many people you'll get?

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:33.110
I think they've done a really good job

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:34.700
in terms of getting people through,

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:38.063
so, I think it's almost
averaging like a thousand a day.

00:03:38.063 --> 00:03:40.990
So, we should have results
sometime next week,

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:42.610
and then we do it all over again.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:44.430
Because you want to sort of test once,

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:46.550
and then test a couple of weeks later.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:48.070
Yeah, because seroconversion,

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:49.520
which is what happens when your body

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:52.858
decides to develop antibodies,
doesn't happen overnight.

00:03:52.858 --> 00:03:56.330
It takes some time after your
body's exposed to the virus

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:58.800
to be able to develop antibodies.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.988
So, we want to be able to catch that.

00:04:00.988 --> 00:04:03.990
That's why we want to wait
at least 14 days between,

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:06.050
to make sure if someone has been infected

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:09.040
that they seroconverted,
and we can identify it.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.220
And then, so, you guys have
a couple of things going on.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:12.810
I mean, so not only are you doing this,

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:17.400
you also, back when
SARS was a huge concern,

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.170
you've done vaccine work around that,

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:21.150
and so, it seems like you
were pretty quickly able

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:23.890
to spin up some tests
around a possible vaccine

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.979
here, for the novel coronavirus.

00:04:27.979 --> 00:04:31.260
So, we've tested a number
of different constructs.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.380
We're actually looking at them right now.

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:34.440
They look pretty promising.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:35.800
They're in animals.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.820
And our hope is to get them
into humans early summer.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:40.390
Are we any closer to sort of knowing

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:43.350
when we'll get the data
from the animal test?

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.001
It's still ongoing.

00:04:45.001 --> 00:04:49.120
And then, Lou, not to put you on the spot,

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:50.770
but, I know you were saying that,

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:54.560
if the animal test came back,
if the data came back good,

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.130
you would sort of think about

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:00.138
using the vaccine at that point,
right, on a personal level?

00:05:00.138 --> 00:05:03.250
I totally, no, not putting
me on the spot at all.

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:05.310
I'm totally down as long
as it doesn't damage

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:07.197
like its FDA path or anything like that.

00:05:07.197 --> 00:05:09.940
Right now, we got a little
bit of interim data,

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:11.940
and I would say they're one step closer

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.030
to having the one or two

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:17.380
that we'd like to pull the
trigger on for human trials,

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:18.920
and I'm like first in line.

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:20.490
I'm before there's a line.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:22.720
And so, you look at this
data from the animal trials,

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:24.390
and then if it looks safe,

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:27.550
you can kind of progress
immediately in the human trials?

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:29.110
Yeah, then it's really about

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:32.370
how the FDA wants to deal
with these trials, right?

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:34.680
But these are emergency times,
these are unprecedented.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:36.560
So, all we know is that
we're going to try to get

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:40.420
the best vaccine into humans
right now, make sure it's safe.

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:43.440
And one of the benefits of
our platform in our company

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:44.937
is that we have scaled up,

00:05:44.937 --> 00:05:47.640
we have a commercially validated platform.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:50.040
I think, scaling up and delivering

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:51.330
is gonna be just as difficult

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:53.767
as finding a really efficacious vaccine,

00:05:53.767 --> 00:05:57.493
that can be distributed to the masses.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:05.830
It's hard to keep track of
all the different vaccines

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:08.270
and approaches that are
being taken right now.

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:10.930
But how optimistic should people be?

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:13.030
'Cause, I just don't want to
give people false hope or,

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:15.950
I just want to set
expectations in the right spot.

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:18.770
Yeah. So, the example that
I give people is that,

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:19.840
remember a few years ago,

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:22.310
when we're reading about pigs
that were dying in China,

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:24.920
by like the thousands
and millions and stuff?

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:27.580
There was a mutation to
foot and mouth disease.

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:30.090
And within a month of finding out,

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:32.270
then being charged with solving that,

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:34.870
we made 100 million brand new vaccines

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:38.730
to a brand-new strain, that
worked, and solved the problem.

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:42.760
So, the answer is, these things
can go really, really fast.

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:44.840
A lot of things have to work out together,

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:48.170
but I think there's going to
be a lot of push to solve it.

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:49.640
Yeah. I mean, there's a ton of

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:50.730
different vaccines going on.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:52.470
I mean, there's over 20 efforts

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:54.100
that are just listed right now,

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:55.410
and they're probably more efforts

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:56.830
that we don't even know about.

00:06:56.830 --> 00:06:57.830
The good news is that

00:06:57.830 --> 00:06:59.250
there are lots of people working on it,

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:00.890
and when you have lots
of people working on it,

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:02.070
that means more shots on goal,

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:03.850
which means that you should feel good,

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:05.770
and hopeful that something will come out.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:07.810
This is like a challenge for humanity.

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:09.050
I don't care if it's us,

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:11.080
I don't care if it's another company

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:12.140
with a different technology,

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:14.557
I just want it to work
and be able to scale up,

00:07:14.557 --> 00:07:16.890
and for us to get back to a new normal,

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:19.290
where we could still
think about going out,

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:22.010
having 4th of July parties,
having concerts and stuff.

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:23.710
So, I was just thinking about,

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:27.030
like when you get all the
data from San Miguel County,

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:29.790
is that something you would,
like sort of analyze yourself,

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:30.810
or is that something that goes up

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:33.160
on a website that everybody can see?

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:34.504
It's definitely all anonymized.

00:07:34.504 --> 00:07:36.200
The people who are positive

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:38.690
are notified directly by the med center.

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:42.030
So, there will be quarantines,
or like isolation,

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:44.840
based on who's infected,
and then hopefully

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:48.743
the rest of the community can
feel safer about coming out.

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:54.090
One thing that is weird, Ashlee,

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:55.930
that's strange about doing this,

00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:58.280
it's one thing to deploy
it in distant places,

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:00.570
even if you know those places really well,

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:03.680
it's another, when you're
deploying it in like right there,

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:06.180
and everybody's like in it.

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:07.840
It's really amazing.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:08.840
Well, thank you guys,

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:12.220
and I just really appreciate your time.

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:13.380
All right, thanks, Ashlee, appreciate it

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:15.090
Thanks, Ashlee, talk to you later.

